From ae30a08d70cd37909cb188fa9e1551db1584ee85 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 20:43:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 106/294] gallium/pvr: skeleton for a native PowerVR Rogue
 driver

Milestone 1 of a native GL driver for Imagination PowerVR (BXM-4-64): a driver that builds
in-tree and registers a pipe_screen. There is NO draw path, no rasterisation and no resource
management, and none is claimed - a gallium driver of this class is 10k-30k lines (lima 9410,
v3d 16164, vc4 22645, etnaviv 28589).

Why this exists: Mesa has no PowerVR GL driver, so GL on the open stack is translated
GL -> zink -> Vulkan -> powervr and reaches about 72% of the vendor's native GL (582 vs 809
off-screen on glmark2-es2 at 800x600). zink configuration is exhausted and the Vulkan driver is
at parity, so a native GL driver is the only remaining route.

Wiring follows the ethosu pattern: added to the gallium-drivers list and its meson choices, a
with_gallium_pvr flag, and a subdir block in src/gallium/meson.build.

The reusable half already exists and is deliberately NOT rewritten: pco (25378 lines of NIR-based
PowerVR compiler, confirmed to reference no Vulkan types), src/imagination/common, and the kernel
UAPI. The next step is device enumeration via DRM_IOCTL_PVR_DEV_QUERY, which first needs the UAPI
header moved out of src/imagination/vulkan/winsys/powervr/ into a shared location - a gallium
driver should not include from inside the Vulkan driver.

Builds as: ninja -C build src/gallium/drivers/pvr/libpvr.a
